<h1>Leftists and empathy</h1>
<h2>August 29, 2026</h2>
I look at how leftists with a strong agenda are too blinded by
 resentment to have empathy and compassion.
<a name="2026-08-29-the-offending-comments"> </a>
<h3>The offending comments</h3>
<p>On Reddit, there were some comments about <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-28-webcam.html">an article I touched on in yesterday’s blog</a> 
about a Seattle area
 online pimp.
<p>I found <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-29-Reddit.html">two  postings</a>  
very interesting---these people look to have been exposed to leftist
 propaganda for so long, they see men who consume porn as caricatures of
 evil. They have a hard time fathoming the humanity of the kinds of men
 who are addicted to porn; they see those men as heartless monsters who
 merely see women as sex objects.
<p>They overlook parts of the story which contradict the hateful
 narrative they have created in their head about these men---e.g. they
 overlook the guy who gave one of the young women some $35,000.
<p>“Flaming-Framing” does frame the issue in a very flamebait manner:
<blockquote>
<p>the guy trying to hurt online sex workers by outing them to their
 family wasn’t doing it because it was his “kink”. He was just angry and
 bitter that beautiful women can choose to have sex with other people
 who aren’t him.
</blockquote>
<p>I don’t think that was Yo Yo’s motivation, and the fact that
 “Flaming- framing” think it is means they did not meaningfully read the
 story. I have <a href="AI-summaries/2026-08-29-YoYo.html">an AI summary of just the Yo Yo part of the story they were commenting on</a>, 
and there is, to put it mildly, a lot more nuance that
 “Flaming-Framing” didn’t see.
<p>There is not anything in the article to support the notion that he
 doxxed these young women because he was “angry and bitter that
 beautiful women can choose to have sex with other people”. Indeed,
 reading the original copy, it also mentions that “Some speculated that
 he was an off-duty Baptist preacher”.
<p>I think Yo Yo is, based on the article, better described as a split
 man. He undoubtedly found the women beautiful and desirable, but he
 also felt they could contribute more to the world than be virtual sex
 toys for lonely men. I would say he wanted those women to do better,
 and he told their families about what these women were doing so that
 the women could make better life choices. It appears he got the girls
 to disclose their most intimate secrets to him because, yes, he did
 deep down truly care for them.
<p>He didn’t tell the families of these young women about their choice
 to be cam girls to hurt the women because of some imaginary resentment
 from these women not sleeping with him. He told the families to <em>
help the girls</em>.
<p>It would seem that this “Flaming-Framing” character has a lot of
 hatred towards men. She laments that “our culture is really
 misogynistic” in <a href="https://archive.ph/Xf3es#selection-1935.10-1935.44">another comment</a>.
<p>Because of this hatred, she read the story about someone who, in his
 own way, did have a lot of care for these women who were online
 camgirls, and without any evidence decides he just hates women because
 they won’t sleep with him. Her hatred stops her from having empathy and
 compassion.
<a name="2026-08-29-the-problem-with-social-media"> </a>
<h3>The problem with social media</a> 
</h3>
<p>The problem with social media, especially Reddit, it that it’s full
 of people like “Flaming-Framing” who are too blinded by their hatred
 and ideology to have empathy for people.


